  "body": "Wouldn't it be nice if there was a reserved attribute, say, `js-selector`, so this would be possible:\r\n```js\r\ndocument.querySelectorAll('[js-selector=\"foo\"]');\r\n```\r\nSince some developers doesn't like to use `data-attribute` to store stuff like that, I personally, all of my colleagues (whom I've been managed to interview) and, I guess, many more other developers, struggling with an issue: how to select DOM-elements in a way that was implied by specification writers? So, as you may know (or google) there are three most common ways to do so:\r\n1. `js-` prefix in CSS-classes name (`<div class=\"foo js-selector-class\">`)\r\n2. data-attributes (`<div class=\"foo\" data-js-selector>`)\r\n3. Some other attributes like `rel`  (`<div class=\"foo\" rel=\"js-selector\">`)\r\n\r\nIt seems like none of those ways was not implied by the specification, so I'm asking you, guys, to extend it",
